In KiCad, set the drill file origin to the bottom left of the PCB. Also select
"use drill/place file origin" during fabrication output creation.

In Kicad, configure the alignment hole to be Through Hole and assign it to the
net of the copper it is placed on. For example, GND. That way, there won't be
any extra paths to provide clearance around them.

When generating the fabrication outputs, remove all copper pours (Ctrl-B). This
is necessary to avoid unnecessary isolation milling on the board outline.

Also, in the fabrication output generation dialog box, uncheck the box that says
"Use extended X2 format (recommended). This will prevent pcb2gcode from spitting
out lots of warnings like "Unknown RS-274X extension found ..."

In pcb2gcode, use a zwork = -0.05 and for a 30 degree, 0.1 mm tip diameter
spiral V cutter, use mill-diameters = 0.15359

Configure the CNC to not reduce stepper currents during idle (M906)

When moving the spindle to the starting position (home), move it to the bottom
left of the PCB to match what KiCad is displaying for the drill file origin.

Place the blank PCB such that the new alignment holes will not coincide with
previous alignment holes on the sacrificial MDF plate.

Give a unique diameter for the alignment hole (for example, 3.01 mm for a 3 mm
hole). Specify this inside the millproject file. Also specify the hole depth

alignment-hole-diameter = 3.01
alignment-hole-depth = -7

Make sure the zero-start flag is set in millproject. This is important to
guarantee that the top and bottom layers match up.

zero-start = 1

The isolation milling needs to be done in the correct order. Check the output of
pcb2gcode. If the output says that the PCB should be cut and drilled from the
FRONT side, then isolation milling must start from the BACK side. That way, when
processing of the BACK side is complete, the board can be turned around using
the alignment pins and all remaining work can be done on the FRONT side.

Let's assume here that pcb2gcode has indicated that PCB cut and drill is to be
done on the FRONT side:

First do "back" isolation milling, then do "milldrill" which also includes the
alignment holes. Then turn the board around (in the left-right direction) and do
"front" isolation milling. And finally do "drill". This sequence is important,
because each isolation milling is also doing surface mapping, and for this to
work, there must be no holes drilled yet.
